1
00:01:33,210 --> 00:01:37,110
and whether or not that does not make it difficult.

2
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:05,810
Care to add anything on Eurodollars?

3
00:04:05,810 --> 00:04:06,990
No, I'm just learning.

4
00:04:09,970 --> 00:04:56,860
Well, I do not pose as a China expert,

5
00:04:57,870 --> 00:05:01,950
and I'm not even, despite having lived most of this century,

6
00:05:02,090 --> 00:05:03,550
an expert on revolutions,

7
00:05:03,550 --> 00:05:07,730
so this is probably the one thing one learns in this century.

8
00:05:08,390 --> 00:05:11,550
But it seems to me quite unlikely

9
00:05:11,550 --> 00:05:16,800
That the present policy of forced draft...

10
00:05:19,100 --> 00:05:43,300
And it is, I think, reasonably clear that Chinese agriculture, and that's 90% of China,

11
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:48,660
is in worse shape than it was in the 50s.

12
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:55,880
That there was a short-lived recovery after the Civil War was over and the fighting stopped,

13
00:05:55,880 --> 00:06:00,080
And then the productivity is not going up.

14
00:06:00,620 --> 00:06:04,420
If anything's going down, the communes don't work.

15
00:06:04,560 --> 00:06:05,140
They never do.

16
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:08,940
Their regime is politically committed to them, however.

17
00:06:13,480 --> 00:06:21,260
And I would say, if you ask what is the most likely scenario,

18
00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:28,380
it is that within 10 years you have a return to an extreme of Stalinism

19
00:06:28,380 --> 00:06:37,670
as the only way, terror as the only way to hold it together or a collapse into

20
00:06:37,670 --> 00:06:46,580
warlords. But I do not, I think the chances of the present trapeze wire

21
00:06:46,580 --> 00:06:54,120
balance working, well how many, how long can you walk the higher wire? Ten minutes?

22
00:06:54,120 --> 00:10:41,080
Fifty minutes? I don't think you can walk it 15 years. Would you?

23
00:10:41,080 --> 00:10:45,040
That's not what I said, I said that all countries won't be rich.

24
00:10:45,200 --> 00:11:15,210
At the risk of sounding like somebody about to enter the next primary, which I am not,

25
00:11:15,770 --> 00:11:24,390
let me say that there is very little proof to the Washington article of the creed that

26
00:11:24,390 --> 00:11:27,350
oil is the cause of our inflation.

27
00:11:27,690 --> 00:11:30,450
I would imagine you would agree very little.

28
00:11:32,070 --> 00:11:37,710
Fundamentally, this is the scapegoat who just happens to be popular,

29
00:11:37,710 --> 00:11:41,870
and we have a long list of such scapegoats,

30
00:11:42,010 --> 00:11:43,370
big steel, big labor,

31
00:11:43,850 --> 00:11:48,410
and inflation is caused by government fundamentally.

32
00:11:49,270 --> 00:11:52,940
But there is very little doubt

33
00:11:52,940 --> 00:11:56,850
that the hike in petroleum prices

34
00:11:56,850 --> 00:12:02,770
hit the rapidly developing countries very hard.

35
00:12:03,920 --> 00:12:05,440
We in the developed world,

36
00:12:05,440 --> 00:12:07,840
I think I'm going to make myself unpopular,

37
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:13,920
I don't know whether we, on balance, benefited from OPEC or paid a little,

38
00:12:14,180 --> 00:12:17,880
but fundamentally we put things from one pocket into the other.

39
00:12:19,050 --> 00:12:20,350
Where do they put their money?

40
00:12:21,010 --> 00:12:25,470
Either into deposits in the developed world,

41
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:31,780
or the only thing of my prediction that hasn't come true when OPEC came on,

42
00:12:31,860 --> 00:12:36,040
I said, finally the French will find the market for the Concorde.

43
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:41,580
he was over optimistic

44
00:12:41,580 --> 00:12:44,520
which I really am

45
00:12:44,520 --> 00:12:47,400
but Kuwait you know

46
00:12:47,400 --> 00:12:49,880
you've got 50 square miles of sand

47
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:51,700
absolute dryness

48
00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:54,360
ideal place to store concords

49
00:12:54,360 --> 00:12:59,100
but they bought everything else

50
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:05,360
and basically in terms of purchasing power

51
00:13:06,280 --> 00:13:13,590
They don't get more money now than they got in 1973 in terms of purchasing power,

52
00:13:13,590 --> 00:13:18,350
both because the dollar is worth half of what it was then

53
00:13:18,350 --> 00:13:24,010
and because in terms of capital goods, which they mostly buy,

54
00:13:24,490 --> 00:13:29,230
the price increases have been much faster than the rate of dollar depreciation.

55
00:13:29,510 --> 00:13:31,290
So they don't get very much more.

56
00:13:31,790 --> 00:13:35,390
They have, however, an enormous amount of liquidity.

57
00:13:35,390 --> 00:13:38,590
they don't know what to do with, where they don't have population.

58
00:13:41,010 --> 00:13:43,650
And frankly, I'm not an economist.

59
00:13:44,050 --> 00:13:48,510
I'm a way of an old semi-historian.

60
00:13:48,510 --> 00:13:51,190
And if there's one lesson in history,

61
00:13:52,260 --> 00:13:56,380
it's that wealth that isn't being put to productive use

62
00:13:56,380 --> 00:14:00,100
is being expropriated very fast,

63
00:14:00,640 --> 00:14:04,020
either by a gang of bandits

64
00:14:04,020 --> 00:14:11,580
or by the people, you know, that old story of the man, the rich man's son who had the money

65
00:14:11,580 --> 00:14:15,760
who went into partnership with the poor man's son who had the knowledge

66
00:14:15,760 --> 00:14:21,820
and ten years later the rich man's son had the experience and the poor man's son had the money

67
00:14:21,820 --> 00:14:26,460
and that's the way it goes

68
00:14:26,460 --> 00:14:33,370
and I do not believe that there is an exception to that rule

69
00:14:33,370 --> 00:14:48,780
and that that money will be a great blessing for countries without population, without skill, without willingness and ability to work.

70
00:14:49,400 --> 00:14:52,780
I think they're only going to lose it sooner or later.

71
00:14:53,480 --> 00:15:02,440
The only question is whether it will be a horrible end or a whimper, but I don't think there is a possible exception to this.

72
00:15:02,440 --> 00:15:08,060
In fact, this is not the lesson of morality, but of history.

73
00:15:08,680 --> 00:23:37,240
Assuming you think there's a need to change it.

74
00:23:37,960 --> 00:23:41,020
Let me say productivity we have had.

75
00:23:43,770 --> 00:23:48,200
A hundred years ago, not quite, but almost a year,

76
00:23:48,940 --> 00:23:52,220
in the last unfinished volume of Das Kapital,

77
00:23:52,460 --> 00:23:55,740
Marx predicted the imminent demise of the system

78
00:23:55,740 --> 00:24:02,150
because of the inexorable law of the decreasing productivity of capital.

79
00:24:02,830 --> 00:24:04,970
And when he meant imminent, he meant imminent,

80
00:24:05,170 --> 00:24:09,090
because in a letter in which he was asked to explain the term,

81
00:24:09,630 --> 00:24:14,360
he said, I will not see it, I am already 70,

82
00:24:15,470 --> 00:24:19,910
but Friedrich Engels, who is 20 years younger, will live to see it.

83
00:24:20,770 --> 00:24:23,510
So that meant before 1900.

84
00:24:25,250 --> 00:24:27,270
And that was just the time.

85
00:24:27,270 --> 00:24:32,870
And he, in that sense, only said what every economist of the 19th century,

86
00:24:32,870 --> 00:24:38,770
almost everyone, except a few heretics, would have considered axiomatic.

87
00:24:39,780 --> 00:24:43,960
And just at that time, as is common with predictions of economists,

88
00:24:44,500 --> 00:24:51,320
things turned around, and the secular increase in the productivity

89
00:24:51,320 --> 00:24:56,040
both of labor and of capital began of labor in this country,

90
00:24:56,540 --> 00:24:59,260
where in those very years Frederick Taylor discovered

91
00:24:59,260 --> 00:25:04,060
that there is an alternative to working harder, which is working smarter.

92
00:25:04,860 --> 00:25:10,960
And in Europe at that time, the first modern banker, George Siemens of the Deutsche Bank,

93
00:25:11,100 --> 00:25:14,220
discovered that one can work on the productivity of money.

94
00:25:14,980 --> 00:25:20,720
And as a result of those two, for a hundred years, we had a steady increase in productivity

95
00:25:20,720 --> 00:25:27,900
to the point where when I was a very young man and allowed to sit in the corner in the

96
00:25:27,900 --> 00:25:33,660
Keynes seminar, Keynes, when being asked why is there no productivity in your model, we

97
00:25:33,660 --> 00:25:41,100
didn't say model in your system, said, because you can't take the continuing increase in

98
00:25:41,100 --> 00:25:46,680
productivity for granted as long as the economy is stable.

99
00:25:47,320 --> 00:25:48,940
And we know this ain't true.

100
00:25:50,200 --> 00:25:58,130
Around 1860, productivity began to turn down in all developed countries, by the way, of

101
00:25:58,130 --> 00:26:06,170
all resources, of money, of key physical resources and of people. And we don't know the reasons

102
00:26:06,170 --> 00:26:12,090
or you can speculate whether it's a shift to the service economy or whether that's because

103
00:26:12,090 --> 00:26:22,540
all of your good people got MBAs, which I think is a very likely cause. And I don't

104
00:26:22,540 --> 00:26:29,460
know why. But since it happened, most in the Soviet Union where they have no MBAs,

105
00:26:29,500 --> 00:26:35,140
I think we can rule out this explanation and continue running this school.

106
00:26:35,680 --> 00:26:42,540
And so, whatever it is, we have had now what is more than a cyclical decline in productivity.

107
00:26:43,020 --> 00:26:52,100
And the rate at which they are going down, if you plot that it is on log-log paper, they would all look the same.

108
00:26:52,600 --> 00:26:54,300
There is not much difference.

109
00:26:54,760 --> 00:26:56,100
There is only one exception.

110
00:26:58,150 --> 00:27:02,290
The productivity of labor has been going up in one country, that's Belgium.

111
00:27:02,290 --> 00:27:04,230
for a very simple reason.

112
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:07,720
They have such an insane law on layoffs

113
00:27:07,720 --> 00:27:09,840
that nobody hires anybody.

114
00:27:10,720 --> 00:27:14,620
And as a result, Belgium is so grotesquely understaffed

115
00:27:14,620 --> 00:27:16,400
that the productivity is going up.

116
00:27:17,360 --> 00:27:21,520
But I don't think that's a shining example of social policy either.

117
00:27:22,980 --> 00:27:25,940
And we have to go to work and reverse it.

118
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,140
But there is no real difference.

119
00:27:29,040 --> 00:27:34,860
The Japanese started from a much higher increase in productivity of labor, by the way.

120
00:27:35,220 --> 00:27:39,300
Productivity of capital in Japan is not particularly good.

121
00:27:39,480 --> 00:27:40,040
It's poor.

122
00:27:41,240 --> 00:27:45,480
There, the Germans are much better, and the French are better still.

123
00:27:45,880 --> 00:27:52,840
But we are all going down at about the same rate, and we are still not bottoming out.

124
00:27:52,840 --> 00:28:00,340
Capital, I think we are beginning to, capital is becoming so scarce and expensive that we

125
00:28:00,340 --> 00:28:08,040
are beginning to be a little better at putting it to work, but not so you notice it much.

126
00:28:08,040 --> 00:28:10,940
So this is a great job to be done.

127
00:28:10,940 --> 00:28:20,080
And we know one thing, macroeconomics do not create productivities.

128
00:28:20,080 --> 00:28:27,940
are created microeconomically by the likes of you in your place of business and not by

129
00:28:27,940 --> 00:28:35,700
the great white father in the Federal Reserve Board. That much we do know. As to savings

130
00:28:35,700 --> 00:28:42,340
rates, let me say the last few years I have been doing the kind of things I was once trained

131
00:28:42,340 --> 00:28:50,120
to do, which is to take popular assumptions and reduce them to quantitative terms and

132
00:28:50,120 --> 00:28:56,060
plotting them, and you get some very interesting things. One question I ask myself is, what

133
00:28:56,060 --> 00:29:07,740
in a period of secular inflation is an inflation-proof investment? There are only two. If you have

134
00:29:07,740 --> 00:29:22,020
enough time, meaning 80 years, then first-rate art. Since 1600, or a little before, when

135
00:29:22,020 --> 00:29:28,090
Leonardo da Vinci invented art as an investment, and he actually did.

136
00:29:29,270 --> 00:29:37,320
Art has been the best investment, simply because despite the great activity of forgers,

137
00:29:37,560 --> 00:29:39,960
the supply of old art is limited.
